# Const-correctness

Mark an **instance** method `const` when it does not change logical object state (including refresh helpers that only update child widgets). That enables calls from `const` contexts.

If the function never uses `this`, do **not** add `const` to the method. Make it `static` or a free function in the anonymous namespace. `static` and `const` (on the method) are mutually exclusive.

```cpp
// BAD — static has no this; trailing const will not compile
static QSize probeVideoDisplaySize(const QString& path) const;

// GOOD — no this: static; callers pass const QString&
static QSize probeVideoDisplaySize(const QString& path);
QString color() const { return colorHex_; }  // instance, read-only
```

Pass read-only objects as `const T&` (`QString`, `QSize`, structs). Pass cheap scalars by value; `const` on a by-value parameter in the definition documents non-mutation.

Prefer `const` locals. Lambdas: capture only what you need. Capture `const T&` (or by value for cheap/const data) for reads; `&` only for locals the lambda mutates.

```cpp
// BAD
auto measure = [&](qreal scale) { return measureLayout(scale, data); };

// GOOD
auto measure = [&data, &homeScore](qreal scale) { return measureLayout(scale); };
auto append = [&filterComplex, &stageCounter](int index, const QString& expr) { ... };
```

Do not mark methods `const` when they assign non-`mutable` members or change interaction state. Use `mutable` only for caches/reentrancy guards.
